window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-7daaec47a2ac640c"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"build-a-hackathon-site\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">构建黑客松活动网站</h1> <p>让参与者能够在一个地方查看日程、报名，并分享他们的作品。</p> <p>将活动简介和日程整理成一个网站，引导参与者完成报名和演示提交，并在活动后通过作品展示区分享项目。</p> <p><a id=\"best-for\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">适合哪些情况</h2> <ul> <li>为团队或组织举办黑客松（Hackathon）活动的组织者。</li> <li>负责注册团队并提交演示作品的参与者。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"starter-prompt\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">起步提示词</h2> <p>构建黑客松活动网站</p> <p>建议推理级别：<code>medium</code></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">使用 @Sites，根据以下活动简介和日程为[hackathon name]（黑客松名称）构建活动网站。参与者都属于我们的工作区。\n\n资料来源：\n- 活动简介和组织者联系方式：[attached file or document link]（附件或文档链接）。\n- 日程：[file or spreadsheet link and tab]（文件或电子表格链接及工作表标签）。\n- 活动日期和时区：[date and time zone]（日期和时区）。\n- 团队规则和提交要求：[document link]（文档链接）。\n- 提交截止时间和逾期提交规则：[confirmed details]（已确认的详细信息）。\n- 作品展示区可见范围：[when submitted projects should appear and who can see them]（提交的项目何时显示、哪些人可以查看）。\n- 评审标准和参与者资源：[links or none]（链接或无）。\n\n先确认你能读取哪些资料，并总结日程、截止时间、团队规则和必填提交字段。对缺失信息或相互冲突的时间提出问题。只使用提供的活动信息；让参与者自行选择项目，不要推荐项目创意或示例用例。\n\n构建活动概览、日程、参与者说明、资源列表和组织者联系信息。让参与者容易找到下一场活动、团队注册和项目提交入口。显示活动日期及时区、活动安排和准确的提交截止时间。将截止时间和帮助联系人放在提交表单附近。链接到原始资源，并说明网站使用的是经过审核的活动信息副本。\n\n让参与者先注册团队名称和成员，然后再回来添加项目标题、简短说明和演示链接。区分已保存的草稿和已提交的项目，并说明缺少哪些必填字段。根据提供的可见性规则在作品展示区展示已提交的项目。要求用户登录，并在服务器端验证团队成员身份，确保成员只能编辑自己团队的条目。启用团队编辑之前，询问如何确认成员身份；不能把用户输入的姓名当作身份凭据。在访问之间保存注册和提交内容。\n\n分开保存活动信息、团队注册和项目记录，确保修改日程不会覆盖提交内容。验证必填字段和演示链接。遵守所提供的截止时间规则；如果缺少规则，请先询问。不要编辑源文档或发送邀请。\n\n使用两支虚构团队进行测试：注册、提交、编辑、重新加载，并尝试直接编辑另一支团队的条目。检查截止时间逻辑和手机布局。活动开始前删除测试记录。将网站设为仅对我们的工作区开放，并在发布前展示已保存的预览、测试结果和未解决的问题。\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"related-links\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">关联文档</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-56e8c36f028eb49f?anchor=save-records-and-progress\">保存注册和提交内容</a></li> <li><a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/codex/sites/publishing-and-sharing#choose-who-can-visit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">选择谁可以访问（需联网）</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>如需完整文档索引，请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 末尾追加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该页面的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"before-you-start\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">开始之前</h2> <p>为黑客松参与者提供一个链接，让他们在活动前、活动期间和活动结束后都能使用。在网站中查看日程、注册团队、提交演示作品，并浏览其他人制作的项目。组织者还可以使用同一个作品展示区开展演示，并在活动后分享成果。</p> <p>收集通常会发送给参与者的信息：</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>资料来源</th> <th>示例</th> <th>可提供的内容</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>活动简介</td> <td>包含活动日期、时区、地点和组织者联系方式的文档</td> <td>活动概览和帮助信息</td> </tr> <tr> <td>日程</td> <td>包含开始时间、结束时间、活动、地点或会议链接的电子表格</td> <td>活动安排</td> </tr> <tr> <td>参与者指南</td> <td>团队说明、规则、评审标准、提交截止时间和资源链接</td> <td>如何加入和提交作品的说明</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>确定参与者需要提交什么，以及项目应该何时显示在作品展示区。例如，你可以收集项目标题、团队制作内容的简短说明和演示链接。按照活动规则决定项目是立即显示，还是在提交截止后显示。</p> <p><a id=\"example-a-one-day-team-hackathon\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_6\">示例：一日团队黑客松活动</h3> <p>在这个假设性的活动中，参与者 9:00 集合、9:30 组队、16:00 前提交项目，并于 16:30 开始演示。所有时间均使用活动公布的时区。参与者打开网站查找上午会议链接并注册团队，然后稍后返回补充项目说明和演示链接。演示期间，组织者从作品展示区打开每个已提交的项目。</p> <p>请将示例时间替换为已经确认的日程。项目由参与者自行选择；网站只提供他们参与活动所需的信息和步骤。</p> <p><a id=\"connect-your-sources\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_7\">连接资料来源</h2> <ol> <li>在构建对话中附上活动简介和日程。如果这些资料位于已连接的应用中，请询问 ChatGPT 当前有哪些连接可用，然后提供准确的文档链接和电子表格工作表名称，并完成它要求的连接步骤。例如，如果相关连接可用，请提供 Google Drive 或 SharePoint 中的 “Team hackathon brief” 文档和 “Agenda” 电子表格工作表。会议室和参与者资源可以使用普通链接。</li> <li>让 ChatGPT 总结日程、截止时间、团队规则和必填提交字段。确认活动日期和时区。使用起始提示词之前，先解决相互冲突的截止时间。</li> <li>使用经过审核的信息副本构建网站，并保留指向原始资源的链接。将团队注册和项目提交保存在网站中，确保它们在不同访问之间仍然可用。除非添加实时连接，否则源日程的更改需要单独更新网站。</li> <li>如果需要从已连接的数据源加载日程，请让 ChatGPT 验证该连接是否能够读取它。将网站设为仅对工作区开放，并使用参与者自己的账户进行测试。有关连接流程，请参阅<a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections#build-a-site-with-connected-data\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">数据与连接</a>（官网章节，需联网） <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">本地页面（本地尚未收录）</a>。</li> </ol> <p>请确认参与者使用自己的账户可以打开会议链接、说明和演示链接。你可以先使用附件和普通资源链接；注册和提交功能不要求实时连接。</p> <p><a id=\"how-to-use-it\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">使用方法</h2> <ol> <li>确认活动时间和说明</li> </ol> <p>使用起始提示词和活动材料。打开预览，并对照已批准的活动简介检查。确认日期、时区、各场活动时间、提交截止时间和链接。参与者应能找到接下来去哪里、如何加入以及需要提交什么，而无需询问组织者。将截止时间和帮助联系人放在提交说明附近。</p> <ol> <li>注册团队并提交演示</li> </ol> <p>注册一个虚构测试团队，然后返回网站添加项目标题、说明和演示链接。检查从注册到提交的完整流程：成员是否能看出哪些内容已保存、哪些仍缺失，以及项目是否已经提交？确认项目会按照活动的可见性规则显示在项目集中。</p> <p>编辑条目并重新加载页面，确认更改已保存。使用第二个团队账户测试，并确认该账户不能编辑第一支团队的条目，包括通过直接链接访问时。检查截止时间到达后的行为，并在活动开始前删除测试条目。</p> <ol> <li>在黑客松期间和之后使用网站</li> </ol> <p>检查受众范围，并为参与者发布网站。在活动邀请中加入链接，并请同事尝试在没有你帮助的情况下查找日程和提交说明。活动开始前，根据反馈澄清容易产生疑问的步骤。</p> <p>演示期间，可以从作品展示区打开每个项目，并同时查看团队说明和演示链接。活动结束后，继续向目标受众开放作品展示区，让大家能够查看错过的项目。使用参与者账户检查演示链接，并在更新活动信息时保留已保存的提交内容。</p> <p><a id=\"take-it-further\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">进一步优化</h2> <p><a id=\"update-the-agenda-without-losing-submissions\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_10\">更新日程，同时保留提交内容</h3> <p>更改活动时间或地点时，不要覆盖已注册的团队、已提交的项目或其访问规则。</p> <p><strong>提示词：</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Read the revised agenda from [document link, spreadsheet tab, or attached file]. Show the differences in session times, locations, and the submission deadline before applying them. Keep team registrations, project submissions, and their access rules unchanged. Flag conflicting dates or missing time zones. After I confirm the changes, update the agenda and show a preview before publishing.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">请读取 [document link, spreadsheet tab, or attached file] 中修订后的议程。在应用变更之前，先展示各场次时间、地点和提交截止日期的差异。保持团队报名、项目提交及其访问规则不变。标出日期冲突或缺失的时区。等我确认变更后，再更新议程，并在发布前展示预览。</div></div> <p><a id=\"add-a-submission-countdown\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_11\">添加提交倒计时</h3> <p>使用活动时区显示截止时间，并说明截止时间过后会发生什么。</p> <p><strong>提示词：</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Add a countdown to the confirmed submission deadline on [date and time] in [event time zone]. Keep the exact date, time, and time zone visible beside it. Show a clear message after the deadline instead of negative time. Follow the event's agreed late-submission rule; don't change whether submissions are accepted without asking. Test before, at, and after the deadline.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">请为已经确认的提交截止时间添加倒计时：时间为 [date and time]，时区为 [event time zone]。在倒计时旁持续显示准确的日期、时间和时区。截止后显示清楚的提示，不要显示负数时间。遵循活动已经约定的逾期提交规则；未经询问，不要改变是否接受提交的设置。测试截止之前、截止当时和截止之后的情况。</div></div>","en":"<h1 id=\"build-a-hackathon-site\">Build a hackathon site</h1> <p>Give participants one place to find the agenda, register, and share what they built.</p> <p>Turn your event brief and agenda into a site that guides participants through registration and demo submission, then keeps their projects available in a gallery.</p> <h2 id=\"best-for\">Best for</h2> <ul> <li>Organizers running a hackathon for their team or organization.</li> <li>Participants registering a team and submitting a demo.</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"starter-prompt\">Starter prompt</h2> <p>Build a hackathon site</p> <p>Suggested reasoning effort：<code>medium</code></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Use @Sites to build a hackathon site for [hackathon name] using the event brief and agenda below. The participants belong to our workspace.\n\nSources:\n- Event brief and organizer contact: [attached file or document link].\n- Agenda: [file or spreadsheet link and tab].\n- Event date and time zone: [date and time zone].\n- Team rules and submission requirements: [document link].\n- Submission deadline and late-submission rule: [confirmed details].\n- Project gallery visibility: [when submitted projects should appear and who can see them].\n- Judging criteria and participant resources: [links or none].\n\nFirst confirm which sources you can read and summarize the schedule, deadline, team rules, and required submission fields. Ask about gaps or conflicting times. Use only the supplied event details. Leave project choice to participants; don't suggest project ideas or example use cases.\n\nBuild an event overview, agenda, participant instructions, resource list, and organizer contact section. Make it easy to find the next session, team registration, and project submission. Show the event date and time zone with the schedule and exact submission deadline. Keep the deadline and help contact near the submission form. Link to original resources and explain that this is a reviewed copy of the event information.\n\nLet participants register a team with its name and members, then return to add a project title, short description, and demo link. Distinguish saved drafts from submitted projects and explain any missing required fields. Show submitted projects in a gallery according to the supplied visibility rule. Require sign-in and enforce team membership on the server so members can edit only their own team's entry. Ask how membership should be confirmed before enabling team edits; don't use a typed name as proof of identity. Save registrations and submissions between visits.\n\nKeep event information, team registrations, and project records separate so agenda edits preserve submissions. Validate required fields and demo links. Follow the supplied deadline rule; ask if it is missing. Don't edit source documents or send invitations.\n\nTest with two fictional teams: register, submit, edit, reload, and try to edit the other team's entry directly. Check the deadline behavior and mobile layout. Identify and remove test records before the event. Keep the site private to our workspace and show a saved preview, test results, and unresolved questions before publishing.\n</code></pre> <h2 id=\"related-links\">Related links</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-56e8c36f028eb49f?anchor=save-records-and-progress\">Save registrations and submissions</a></li> <li><a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/codex/sites/publishing-and-sharing#choose-who-can-visit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Choose who can visit (online)</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"before-you-start\">Before you start</h2> <p>Give hackathon participants a link they can use before, during, and after the event. Build a site where they can find the schedule, register a team, submit a demo, and explore what others made. Organizers can use the same project gallery to run demos and share the results afterward.</p> <p>Gather the information you would normally send participants:</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Source</th> <th>Example</th> <th>What it contributes</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Event brief</td> <td>A document with event date, time zone, location, and organizer contact</td> <td>The event overview and help information</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Agenda</td> <td>A spreadsheet with start time, end time, session, and location or meeting link</td> <td>The schedule</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Participant guide</td> <td>Team instructions, rules, judging criteria, submission deadline, and resource links</td> <td>Instructions for joining and submitting</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Decide what participants need to submit and when projects should appear in the gallery. For example, you might collect a title, a short description of what the team built, and a demo link. Use the event's rules to decide whether entries appear immediately or when submissions close.</p> <h3 id=\"example-a-one-day-team-hackathon\">Example: A one-day team hackathon</h3> <p>In this hypothetical event, participants meet at 9:00, form teams at 9:30, submit projects by 16:00, and begin demos at 16:30. All times use the event's stated time zone. A participant opens the site to find the morning meeting link, registers their team, then returns later to add the project description and demo. During presentations, the organizer opens each submitted project from the gallery.</p> <p>Replace the sample times with your confirmed schedule. Participants choose what to build; the site gives them the information and steps they need to take part.</p> <h2 id=\"connect-your-sources\">Connect your sources</h2> <ol> <li>Attach the event brief and agenda in the build conversation. If they're in a connected app, ask ChatGPT which connections are available, then provide the exact document links and spreadsheet tab name. Complete any connection step it requests. For example, supply the “Team hackathon brief” document and “Agenda” spreadsheet tab from Google Drive or SharePoint, if the relevant connection is available. Use ordinary links for the meeting room and participant resources.</li> <li>Ask ChatGPT to summarize the schedule, deadline, team rules, and required submission fields. Confirm the event date and time zone. Resolve conflicting deadlines before using the starter prompt.</li> <li>Build from a reviewed copy of these details, with links to original resources. Keep team registrations and project submissions in the site so they remain available between visits. Changes to the source agenda need a separate site update unless you add a live connection.</li> <li>If you need the agenda to load from a connected source, ask ChatGPT to verify that the connection can read it. Keep the site private to your workspace and test with a participant's own account. Follow <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections#build-a-site-with-connected-data\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">data and connections</a> (official section, online) <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Local page (not in this local snapshot)</a>.</li> </ol> <p>Check that participants can open meeting links, instructions, and demo links with their own accounts. You can start with attached files and ordinary resource links; a live connection isn't required for registration and submissions.</p> <h2 id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it</h2> <ol> <li>Confirm the event clock and instructions</li> </ol> <p>Use the starter prompt with your event materials. Open the preview and compare it with the approved brief. Check the date, time zone, session times, submission deadline, and links. A participant should be able to find where to go next, how to join, and what to submit without asking an organizer. Keep the deadline and help contact near the submission instructions.</p> <ol> <li>Register a team and submit a demo</li> </ol> <p>Register a fictional test team, then return to add a project title, description, and demo link. Check the full path from registration to a submitted entry: can members tell what's saved, what's still missing, and whether their project has been submitted? Confirm that it appears in the gallery according to your event's visibility rule.</p> <p>Edit the entry and reload to confirm the changes were saved. Test with a second team account and confirm it can't edit the first team's entry, including through a direct link. Check what happens at the submission deadline and remove test entries before the event.</p> <ol> <li>Use the site during and after the hackathon</li> </ol> <p>Review the audience and publish the site for your participants. Add the link to the event invitation and ask a colleague to find the agenda and submission instructions without your help. Before the event, use their feedback to clarify any confusing steps.</p> <p>During demos, use the gallery to open each project and see the team's description alongside its demo link. After the event, keep the gallery available to the intended audience so people can revisit projects they missed. Check demo links from a participant's account, and update event information without replacing the saved submissions.</p> <h2 id=\"take-it-further\">Take it further</h2> <h3 id=\"update-the-agenda-without-losing-submissions\">Update the agenda without losing submissions</h3> <p>Change session times or locations without overwriting registered teams, submitted projects, or their access rules.</p> <p><strong>Prompt:</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Read the revised agenda from [document link, spreadsheet tab, or attached file]. Show the differences in session times, locations, and the submission deadline before applying them. Keep team registrations, project submissions, and their access rules unchanged. Flag conflicting dates or missing time zones. After I confirm the changes, update the agenda and show a preview before publishing.\n</code></pre> <h3 id=\"add-a-submission-countdown\">Add a submission countdown</h3> <p>Make the deadline visible in the event's time zone and explain what happens when it passes.</p> <p><strong>Prompt:</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Add a countdown to the confirmed submission deadline on [date and time] in [event time zone]. Keep the exact date, time, and time zone visible beside it. Show a clear message after the deadline instead of negative time. Follow the event's agreed late-submission rule; don't change whether submissions are accepted without asking. Test before, at, and after the deadline.\n</code></pre>"};
